  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1122 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nA sfc trof axis will prevail across western Kansas today. Ahead\nof the boundary, breezy and gusty southwesterly winds are\nexpected during the day. Highs today will range from the upper\n70s across the northwest zones, to the lower 80s across south-\ncentral Kansas. The sfc boundary will move slightly eastward by\nearly evening. Light northerly winds are expected in its wake \nby 00Z. This is particularly true for Garden City and Hays. \nSignificant fire weather concerns are not expected even with the\nwarm temperatures and breezy winds. Afternoon relative humidity\nlevels are expected to remain in the moderate levels, which \nwill prevent and major fire wx concerns. \n\nOtherwise, the boundary will then shift southward late tonight \ninto Thursday morning. North of the front, a good moist/NE to E \nupslope flow pattern looks to develop by Thursday morning. This \nis conceptually a good weather pattern that favors stratus and \nfog. Have gone ahead after looking at some CAMs and inserted fog\nmainly north of Highway 50 and 56. In addition, Thursday \nafternoon highs look to have big bust potential. In this \nparticular weather pattern, the fog and stratus tends to linger\nlonger than what many models indicate. Have collaborated with \nGLD and decreased highs in the north to account for the stratus \nand fog. If the fog and stratus is widespread, highs may\nstruggle to reach above 60F in the north. This would be\nsignificantly cooler than what the current NBM indicates.\n\nAttention then turns to Friday into Saturday morning. Moisture\nadvection is expected Friday with PWATs increasing over 1.00\". A\nmid level trof is expected to move across the region Friday\nevening. This will set the stage stage for rain showers. There\nmight be a rumble of thunder, however, both lapse rates and\ninstability look to be on the very marginal side. The best\nmoisture profile looks to remain across the southeast counties.\nAs such, the highest NBM pops are in this region. Current NBM\nhighs Friday may be a few degrees too warm, with all the\nprecipitation and clouds lingering. Precipitation amounts will\nvary, but the southeast counties may seem amounts of around 0.5\"\nwith amounts around 0.10 to 0.25\" across the northwest zones.\n\nLong range models and the ensemble counterparts are indicating\nfor the potential for a much stronger weather system to move\nacross the region Monday night into Tuesday morning. The\nensembles are not in agreement though the the absolute\nstrength of the mid level low and where the heaviest\nprecipitation axis will be located. For example, the GEFS keeps \nthe stronger system low farther south than compared with the \nENS. This would shift the precipitation axis farther south than \ncompared to the wetter ENS. This is something to watch. The \nbottom line is that moisture left over from a tropical weather \nsystem (Vance) may lead to unsettled weather next week. In \naddition, if the system is deep and closed, the resultant wind \nfield may be much stronger than what we have seen as of late.",
